Logic for formatting:
- If ns >= 1 second (1_000_000_000): seconds, e.g. "2s" or "1.5s"
- If ns >= 100_000_000 (0.1s): fraction like "0.5s", "0.25s"
- Otherwise: "1/Ns" with N rounded.

Typical implementation:
```kotlin
fun formatShutterSpeed(exposureTimeNs: Long): String {
    if (exposureTimeNs <= 0L) return "--"
    val seconds = exposureTimeNs / 1_000_000_000.0
    return when {
        seconds >= 1.0 -> {
            val whole = seconds.roundToInt()
            if (Math.abs(seconds - whole) < 0.05) "${whole}s" else String.format("%.1fs", seconds)
        }
        exposureTimeNs >= 100_000_000L -> String.format("%.1fs", seconds)  // e.g.: 0.5s
        else -> "1/${(1.0 / seconds).roundToInt()}s"
    }
}
```
roundToInt is imported (kotlin.math.roundToInt). Use String.format — should I use Locale? String.format defaults to the default locale. No problem, or I could use Locale.US. I'll go with String.format(java.util.Locale.US, ...).

